1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
A blockbuster has a $200M budget. The game sets aside $70M before the first hire.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:19,000
This film uses 36 fictional people and 20 films. You can reproduce the choices locally.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Each pick must leave enough money and enough distinct people to fill every remaining slot.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:47,000
The example takes the best thriller fit among the legal cards offered. Total talent spend: $62M.

5
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:57,000
The same fictional film lists both jobs. The graph keeps those source credits on the edge.

6
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Six shared movies reach the 12-point edge cap. The game scores twelve specific roster relationships.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Genre fit, talent, chemistry and hiring efficiency each contribute to the studio score.

8
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The engine adds the unrounded contributions, then rounds the displayed total.

9
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Production is the $70M reserve plus $62M in hires. Marketing adds another $26.4M.

10
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:46,000
The studio receives half the fictional gross. Subtract both costs from receipts to get profit.

11
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Both casts face the same 64 scenarios. The cheaper director reduces median fictional profit by about $6.02M.

12
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Only the hypothetical roster changes. Run python -m scripts.portfolio_tour from backend/ to reproduce this film.

